<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-1-areas-between-curves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Areas Between Curves</title>
  <!-- Session 14 . Tue Oct 13 . Stewart Sec. 6.1 .
       Built from the Section 6.1 instructor handout ("From an Integral to a Picture, and Back")
       in the conversational handout voice. Written 2026-10-22.
       Key move: the strip height is a DISTANCE between boundaries, not a coordinate and not
       "subtract the curve names by habit". Do not open with "split at every intersection";
       let the x vs x^3 contradiction create the need.
       Interactives: assets/jsxgraph/strip-floor.js, strip-between-curves.js,
       signed-cancel.js, strip-direction.js. -->

  <introduction>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-question-areas-between">
      <title>Today's Question</title>
      <p>
        What does one thin piece measure?
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      An integral is not just something to compute. It is a description of a
      picture: what we are adding up, where we start and stop, and which
      variable is changing. Today we read integrals as pictures, and then we go
      backward, from a picture to an integral.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-familiar">
    <title>A familiar integral: can we see its meaning?</title>

    <p>
      Consider
      <md>
        \int_0^2 (3 - x)\,dx.
      </md>
      Read it as the area under <m>y = 3 - x</m>, above the <m>x</m>-axis,
      from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 2</m>. <alert>Do not evaluate yet.</alert>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-strip-floor">
      <caption>One thin strip under <m>y = 3 - x</m>.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-strip-floor" label="interactive-strip-floor"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/strip-floor.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-strip-floor-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Drag <m>x</m> to slide the strip across the region. Read off its top,
            its bottom, and its height. Leave the checkbox alone for now.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-bounds-mean" label="check-6-1-bounds-mean">
      <title>What do the bounds tell us?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>In <m>\int_0^2 (3 - x)\,dx</m>, what are the numbers <m>0</m> and <m>2</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Where the strips start and stop along the <m>x</m>-axis</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. The bounds say where the accumulation begins and ends.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>The smallest and largest heights of the region</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Not quite. The heights run from <m>1</m> to <m>3</m>. The bounds are <m>x</m>-values, not heights.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      A typical strip has height <m>(3 - x) - 0 = 3 - x</m> and width
      <m>\Delta x</m>.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-1-predict-trapezoid" label="ex-6-1-predict-trapezoid">
      <title>Predict</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the area bigger than <m>2</m> and smaller than <m>6</m>? Explain
          using the picture, not a calculation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes. The width is <m>2</m> and every height is between <m>1</m> and
          <m>3</m>. So the region contains the <m>2</m>-by-<m>1</m> rectangle and
          fits inside the <m>2</m>-by-<m>3</m> rectangle: <m>2 \lt A \lt 6</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-trapezoid" label="check-6-1-trapezoid">
      <title>Now evaluate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_0^2 (3 - x)\,dx =</m> <fillin answer="4" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>\left[3x - \frac{x^2}{2}\right]_0^2 = 6 - 2 = 4</m>. Check with geometry: a trapezoid with parallel sides <m>3</m> and <m>1</m>, width <m>2</m>, has area <m>\frac12(3 + 1)(2) = 4</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-integral-information">
      <title>What just happened</title>
      <p>
        An integral tells us <alert>what we accumulate</alert>,
        <alert>where we begin and end</alert>, and <alert>which variable changes</alert>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-bottom-moves">
    <title>What changes when the bottom boundary moves?</title>

    <p>
      Now consider
      <md>
        \int_0^2 \bigl[(3 - x) - 1\bigr]\,dx.
      </md>
      The region now sits between <m>y = 3 - x</m> and <m>y = 1</m>. Check the
      box in <xref ref="fig-strip-floor"/> to raise the floor.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-strip-at-half" label="check-6-1-strip-at-half">
      <title>Read the strip</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Put the strip at <m>x = \frac12</m>. Its height is
          <fillin answer="1.5" mode="number"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. The top is at <m>y = \frac52</m>, the bottom at <m>y = 1</m>, so the height is <m>\frac32</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <numcmp value="2.5" tolerance="0.001"/>
            <feedback><p>That is where the top of the strip is. The strip no longer starts at <m>y = 0</m>. How long is it?</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      So <m>(3 - x) - 1</m> measures the vertical distance from the lower
      boundary to the upper one. <m>3 - x</m> alone is only the top coordinate.
      The part from <m>y = 0</m> to <m>y = 1</m> is no longer in the region.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-floor-area" label="check-6-1-floor-area">
      <title>Predict, then check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Should this area be bigger or smaller than <m>4</m>? Then evaluate:
          <m>\int_0^2 \bigl[(3 - x) - 1\bigr]\,dx =</m> <fillin answer="2" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. Smaller: we removed the <m>2</m>-by-<m>1</m> rectangle underneath, and <m>4 - 2 = 2</m>. It is also a triangle with base <m>2</m> and height <m>2</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-coordinate-not-length">
    <title>A coordinate is not a length</title>

    <p>
      A vertical segment runs from <m>y = -3</m> to <m>y = -1</m>. Its length is
      <m>(-1) - (-3) = 2</m>. Both ends have negative coordinates. The length is
      still positive.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-below-axis" label="check-6-1-below-axis">
      <title>True or false?</title>
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>A region that sits below the <m>x</m>-axis has negative area.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. Geometric area is never negative. Negative coordinates tell us
          <em>where</em> the boundaries are. The difference, top minus bottom,
          tells us how long the strip is.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      One more thing. On <m>[0, 2]</m>, the region between <m>y = 3 - x</m> and
      <m>y = 0</m>, and the region between <m>y = 4 - x</m> and <m>y = 1</m>,
      have the same strip height:
      <m>(3 - x) - 0 = (4 - x) - 1 = 3 - x</m>. The second region is the first
      one shifted up. Same strips, same area.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-height-is-distance">
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>
        The height of a vertical strip is the <alert>distance between its
        boundaries</alert>. Ask <q>Where is it?</q> and <q>How long is it?</q>
        as two separate questions.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-picture-to-integral">
    <title>Can we go backward? From a picture to an integral</title>

    <p>
      Find the area of the region enclosed by
      <md>
        y = x^2 \qquad\text{and}\qquad y = 2x - x^2.
      </md>
      First describe the region. Then build the integral.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-strip-between">
      <caption>A strip between <m>y = 2x - x^2</m> and <m>y = x^2</m>.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-strip-between" label="interactive-strip-between"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/strip-between-curves.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-strip-between-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Slide the strip. Which curve is always on top? Where does the strip
            shrink to nothing?
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>1. My prediction</title>
      <p>
        The region fits inside a unit square, and it does not fill it. So
        <m>0 \lt A \lt 1</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>2. My strip</title>
      <p>
        At <m>x = \frac12</m>, the strip runs from <m>y = \frac14</m> up to
        <m>y = \frac34</m>. Its height is <m>\frac12</m>. In general:
        <md>
          \text{height} = (2x - x^2) - x^2 = 2x - 2x^2, \qquad \text{width} = \Delta x.
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>3. My endpoints</title>
      <p>
        The strip shrinks to nothing where the curves meet:
        <md>
          x^2 = 2x - x^2 \;\Longrightarrow\; 2x(x - 1) = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; x = 0, 1.
        </md>
        So the strips run from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 1</m>, and in between,
        <m>2x - x^2</m> is on top.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>4. My small piece and my integral</title>
      <p>
        One strip has area about <m>(2x - 2x^2)\,\Delta x</m>. Add them up:
        <md>
          A = \int_0^1 \bigl[(2x - x^2) - x^2\bigr]\,dx = \int_0^1 (2x - 2x^2)\,dx.
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-lens-area" label="check-6-1-lens-area">
      <title>Evaluate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>A =</m> <fillin answer="0.3333" mode="number"/> (a decimal is fine).
          Does it fit your prediction?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.002"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>\left[x^2 - \frac23 x^3\right]_0^1 = \frac13</m>, and <m>0 \lt \frac13 \lt 1</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <numcmp value="-0.3333" tolerance="0.002"/>
            <feedback><p>A negative area means the subtraction went the wrong way. Which curve is on top?</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-picture-strip-integral">
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>
        <alert>Picture <m>\to</m> strip <m>\to</m> dimensions <m>\to</m> integral.</alert>
        The integral records how the small pieces build the whole region.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-zero">
    <title>Two shaded pieces: why might an integral give zero?</title>

    <p>
      Look at the region between <m>y = x</m> and <m>y = x^3</m> on
      <m>[-1, 1]</m>. It comes in two pieces. Make a prediction before you
      integrate anything.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-signed-cancel">
      <caption>The two pieces between <m>y = x</m> and <m>y = x^3</m>.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-signed-cancel" label="interactive-signed-cancel"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="1:1"
                   source="jsxgraph/signed-cancel.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-signed-cancel-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Slide the strip from the right piece into the left piece. Watch the
            value of <m>x - x^3</m>, and watch which curve is on top.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-1-two-pieces" label="ex-6-1-two-pieces">
      <title>Predict</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Do the two pieces have the same area? Could the total area be zero?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Same area: rotating the picture <m>180^\circ</m> about the origin swaps
          the two pieces. But the total cannot be zero. Each piece is made of
          strips with positive length.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Now read two strips off the picture:
    </p>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell>top</cell>
        <cell>bottom</cell>
        <cell>strip height</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>-\frac12</m></cell>
        <cell><m>y = -\frac18</m> (on <m>y = x^3</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>y = -\frac12</m> (on <m>y = x</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>\frac38</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\frac12</m></cell>
        <cell><m>y = \frac12</m> (on <m>y = x</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>y = \frac18</m> (on <m>y = x^3</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>\frac38</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
      A student proposes
      <md>
        A = \int_{-1}^{1} (x - x^3)\,dx.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-signed-zero" label="check-6-1-signed-zero">
      <title>What does the student's integral give?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\int_{-1}^{1} (x - x^3)\,dx =</m> <fillin answer="0" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.0001"/>
            <feedback><p>Right, <m>\left[\frac{x^2}{2} - \frac{x^4}{4}\right]_{-1}^{1} = \frac14 - \frac14 = 0</m>. But the picture clearly has area. Something is wrong with the setup, not the arithmetic.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-what-cancelled" label="check-6-1-what-cancelled">
      <title>What cancelled?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Why did the student's integral come out to <m>0</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>The region really has no area</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Look at the picture. Both pieces have strips with positive length.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>On the left piece, <m>x - x^3</m> is negative, so it cancels the right piece</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. On <m>(-1, 0)</m> the curves trade places: <m>x^3</m> is on top. So <m>x - x^3</m> is bottom minus top there, a negative number. At <m>x = -\frac12</m> it is <m>-\frac38</m>, while the strip is <m>\frac38</m> long.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>The bounds should have been <m>0</m> and <m>1</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>That would only count the right piece. The left piece is part of the region too.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-strip-nonnegative">
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>
        A strip has a nonnegative height. Before you use one subtraction
        everywhere, check whether the boundaries trade roles.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-split">
    <title>Let the picture tell us where to split</title>

    <p>
      Solve <m>x = x^3</m>: <m>x(x - 1)(x + 1) = 0</m>, so <m>x = -1, 0, 1</m>.
      At the inside intersection, <m>x = 0</m>, the top and bottom curves swap.
      So describe each piece separately:
    </p>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>interval</cell>
        <cell>top curve</cell>
        <cell>bottom curve</cell>
        <cell>height</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>[-1, 0]</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x^3</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x^3 - x</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>[0, 1]</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x^3</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x - x^3</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
      Then add the areas:
      <md>
        A = \int_{-1}^{0} (x^3 - x)\,dx + \int_{0}^{1} (x - x^3)\,dx.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-split-area" label="check-6-1-split-area">
      <title>Use the symmetry, then evaluate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>A =</m> <fillin answer="0.5" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. The two pieces match, so <m>A = 2\int_0^1 (x - x^3)\,dx = 2\left(\frac14\right) = \frac12</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <numcmp value="0.25" tolerance="0.001"/>
            <feedback><p>That is one piece. There are two.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <fact xml:id="fact-area-between-curves">
      <title>Area between curves</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are continuous and <m>f(x) \ge g(x)</m> on
          <m>[a, b]</m>, the area between them is
          <md>
            A = \int_a^b \underbrace{\bigl[f(x) - g(x)\bigr]}_{\text{strip height}}\,dx.
          </md>
          If the curves trade places, describe each piece separately and add the
          areas.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-below-axis">
    <title>Your turn: both boundaries below the axis</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-1-below-axis" label="ex-6-1-below-axis">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The region lies between <m>y = -x^2 - 1</m> and <m>y = -3</m> for
          <m>-1 \le x \le 1</m>. Sketch it and draw a strip. Should the area be
          positive or negative? Write an integral for the area and explain why
          your strip height is never negative. Do not evaluate.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The curve <m>y = -x^2 - 1</m> is on top. Height
          <m>= (-x^2 - 1) - (-3) = 2 - x^2</m>, so
          <md>
            A = \int_{-1}^{1} (2 - x^2)\,dx.
          </md>
          Since <m>x^2 \le 1</m> on <m>[-1, 1]</m>, every strip is at least
          <m>1</m> long. The area is positive even though every coordinate is
          negative. Note the side boundaries are the given lines <m>x = \pm 1</m>,
          not where the curves cross.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-direction">
    <title>Does the direction of the strip matter?</title>

    <p>
      Consider the region enclosed by
      <md>
        x = y^2 \qquad\text{and}\qquad x = y + 2.
      </md>
      Try strips in both directions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-strip-direction">
      <caption>The region between <m>x = y^2</m> and <m>x = y + 2</m>, sliced two ways.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-strip-direction" label="interactive-strip-direction"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/strip-direction.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-strip-direction-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Start with vertical strips and slide all the way across. Watch what
            the bottom of the strip lands on. Then check the box for horizontal
            strips and slide again.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-1-vertical-strips" label="ex-6-1-vertical-strips">
      <title>Vertical strips</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Look at vertical strips at <m>x = \frac12</m> and at <m>x = 2</m>.
          Which curve gives the top of each strip? Which gives the bottom? Does
          the bottom stay on the same curve all the way across?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          At <m>x = \frac12</m>, both ends are on the parabola:
          <m>y = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt2}</m>. At <m>x = 2</m>, the bottom is on the
          line (<m>y = 0</m>) and the top is on the parabola (<m>y = \sqrt2</m>).
          The top is always <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m>. The bottom is
          <m>y = -\sqrt{x}</m> for <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, then <m>y = x - 2</m>
          for <m>1 \le x \le 4</m>. It changes at <m>(1, -1)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-horizontal-length" label="check-6-1-horizontal-length">
      <title>Horizontal strips</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A horizontal strip at <m>y = 1</m> runs from <m>x = 1</m> to
          <m>x = 3</m>. Its length is <fillin answer="2" mode="number"/>.
          Do the left and right curves ever trade places as you slide from
          bottom to top?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes, <m>3 - 1 = 2</m>. And no, they never trade places: from <m>y = -1</m> to <m>y = 2</m> the left end is always on <m>x = y^2</m> and the right end is always on <m>x = y + 2</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-both-directions">
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>
        Both directions can work. Choose the one that makes the dimensions of
        the pieces easier to describe.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-horizontal">
    <title>Build an integral from horizontal pieces</title>

    <p>
      Stay with <m>x = y^2</m> and <m>x = y + 2</m>, and use horizontal strips.
      <ol>
        <li><p><alert>Dimensions.</alert> Length <m>= x_{\text{right}} - x_{\text{left}} = (y + 2) - y^2</m>. Thickness <m>= \Delta y</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Small piece.</alert> Area <m>\approx \bigl[(y + 2) - y^2\bigr]\,\Delta y</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Bounds.</alert> <m>y^2 = y + 2</m> gives <m>(y - 2)(y + 1) = 0</m>, so <m>y = -1</m> and <m>y = 2</m>. The variable changing is <m>y</m>, so these are the bounds.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Integral.</alert> <m>A = \int_{-1}^{2} \bigl[(y + 2) - y^2\bigr]\,dy</m>.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-1-horizontal-area" label="check-6-1-horizontal-area">
      <title>Evaluate</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>A =</m> <fillin answer="4.5" mode="number"/>. The region fits in a
          <m>4</m>-by-<m>3</m> box. Does your answer fit too?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>\left[\frac{y^2}{2} + 2y - \frac{y^3}{3}\right]_{-1}^{2} = \frac{10}{3} + \frac76 = \frac92</m>, and <m>0 \lt \frac92 \lt 12</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Vertical strips work too. They just need two integrals, because the bottom
      curve changes at <m>x = 1</m>:
      <md>
        A = \int_0^1 2\sqrt{x}\,dx + \int_1^4 \bigl[\sqrt{x} - (x - 2)\bigr]\,dx = \frac43 + \frac{19}{6} = \frac92.
      </md>
      Same region, same area. The two setups only reflect how the strip
      boundaries are described.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dx-dy-table">
      <title>Same reasoning, a different direction</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>strip</cell>
          <cell>length</cell>
          <cell>thickness</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>vertical</cell>
          <cell><m>y_{\text{top}} - y_{\text{bottom}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>dx</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>horizontal</cell>
          <cell><m>x_{\text{right}} - x_{\text{left}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>dy</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-1-exit">
    <title>Exit ticket: make the geometry visible</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-1-exit" label="ex-6-1-exit">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The region is enclosed by <m>x = 1 + y^2</m> and <m>x = 3 - y^2</m>.
          Do not evaluate.
          <ol>
            <li><p>Can the area be negative or zero? Explain without calculating.</p></li>
            <li><p>Choose a strip direction and say why.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write both dimensions of your strip.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the bounds.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write an integral for the area. Do you need more than one?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The area is positive: the region has positive width and height.
          Horizontal strips are easiest, because the left curve is always
          <m>x = 1 + y^2</m> and the right curve is always <m>x = 3 - y^2</m>.
          Length <m>= (3 - y^2) - (1 + y^2) = 2 - 2y^2</m>, thickness
          <m>\Delta y</m>. The curves meet where <m>y^2 = 1</m>, at
          <m>(2, -1)</m> and <m>(2, 1)</m>. One integral is enough:
          <md>
            A = \int_{-1}^{1} (2 - 2y^2)\,dy.
          </md>
          A vertical setup also works, but needs two integrals split at
          <m>x = 2</m>: <m>\int_1^2 2\sqrt{x - 1}\,dx + \int_2^3 2\sqrt{3 - x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-1-final-question" label="ex-6-1-final-question">
      <title>One final question</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why can an integral equal zero even though the area between two curves
          is positive?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          An integral of one fixed difference can have positive and negative parts
          that cancel. Area adds up strip lengths, which are never negative, using
          the correct top and bottom on each part.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-6-1-main-idea">
      <title>Main idea</title>
      <p>
        Read the picture. Draw a piece. Describe its dimensions. Check whether
        that description works across the whole region. Then write the integral
        that adds the pieces.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>
</section>
